# Build-time flags for the deployed build.
#
# VITE_DEMO_IDENTITY lets the application run on its bundled seed data when no
# API answers. That is what makes the deployment browsable: without it every
# route redirects to a sign-in form with no database behind it to authenticate
# against.
#
# It is a deliberate, temporary trade. While it is set:
#
#   - sign-in asks for an email and a password, but the password is not checked,
#     so any password opens the account the email names;
#   - anyone with the URL can therefore become any seeded person; and
#   - the seed session is a `sessionStorage` key, so it can also be set by hand
#     in devtools without touching the form at all.
#
# Two things it does NOT do, which are worth knowing:
#
#   - it does not weaken a build that has an API. A real server refusing a real
#     password is not the same as there being no server, and only the second
#     falls through to the local check — see `noServiceBehind` in SignIn.
#   - it does not reach the database. Every /api route still requires a Better
#     Auth session, and row-level security still scopes every query.
#
# That is acceptable only because every record here is fictional — the salary
# figures are invented, the identity fields are placeholders, and the email
# addresses are at a domain that does not exist.
#
# Delete this line before the database is connected. Real records behind an
# unchecked password is the one thing this must never ship as. A build with the
# flag on says so in the console at startup; see src/lib/demo.ts.
VITE_DEMO_IDENTITY=true
